{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SBJ8t9zhaeif1TGkbuKm6h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted experimental results for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment plan, under the \"best\" tier definition. I need to produce a single YAML block with the specified format.\n\nLet me carefully go through each planned experiment and generate predictions consistent with the \"best\" tier:\n\nKey metrics ranges for \"best\" tier:\n- cwm_generation_per_class_f1: [68, 76]\n- classification_minus_cwm_gap: [11, 19]\n- trace_then_judge_f1: [72, 78]\n\n\n- repo_level_per_class_f1: [80, 86]\n- diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_lift_pct: [5, 10]\n- block_corr_spearman_vs_iid: [0.3, 0.6]\n- per_test_failure_precision: [0.82, 0.9]\n- per_test_failure_recall: [0.78, 0.86]\n\nThe narrative shows classification reformulation achieving roughly 87% F1 versus generation's 72%, supporting a 15-point gap across all three claims. I have the baseline from mvp-b-train with pass_f1=0.871 and fail_f1=0.879 to work from.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the remaining experiments. The cwm-baseline should land around 72 F1 to demonstrate that gap, while trace-then-judge will sit between CWM and classification to show formulation's impact. I still need to run trajectory-level fine-tuning as a planned baseline. Now I'm adding the majority voting approach as another baseline, then structuring the YAML output with result rationales, tables, and figures for each experiment. I'm setting specific performance numbers that stay internally consistent—classification hitting around 87% F1 for both classes, CWM baseline around 72% to show a clear gap, trace-then-judge falling between them, and trajectory-level finetuning performing decently since it's still a classification task but without the per-test granularity. I'm pulling together the experimental results across different model sizes and evaluation approaches—looking at how classification and CWM performance scale, the diagnostic repair lift, precision-recall tradeoffs, and various stratified analyses—now I need to format this cleanly as YAML.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9894,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9894},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd3967e0-8c52-4a08-9b3d-3623462e92c4","uuid":"468c6193-d519-47b1-b478-5c4d2f6443da"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SBJ8t9zhaeif1TGkbuKm6h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Classification reformulation achieves 87% F1 vs generation's ~72%, a 15-point gap confirming that\n  execution simulation is better cast as classification than generation. Three claims fully supported:\n  Claim 1 by large F1 gap + trace-then-judge ablation proving formulation (not just task simplicity) drives\n  the advantage; Claim 2 by diagnostic re-repair producing 7%+ resolve rate lift; Claim 3 by oracle\n  frontier stratification and empirical block-correlation (ρ≈0.45) mutually reinforcing per-test advantage\n  in multi-test instances. EMNLP Main submission, pitched as \"task formulation matters: classification vs\n  generation as a general LLM capability probe.\" P2P regression ~1% reported honestly as a characterization\n  finding that sharpens the deployment decision framework.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"CWM Generation Baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"cwm-baseline\"\n    description: >\n      Train Qwen3-4B LoRA to predict execution output (stdout/stderr + exit code) instead of binary\n      pass/fail, using identical training data and hyperparameters as MVP-B classification model.\n      Evaluate per-class F1 on same held-out split.\n    expected_results:\n      overall_accuracy: 0.735\n      pass_f1: 0.703\n      fail_f1: 0.738\n      pass_precision: 0.721\n      pass_recall: 0.686\n      fail_precision: 0.714\n      fail_recall: 0.763\n      exact_match_output: 0.581\n      token_budget_exhaustion_rate: 0.127\n      string_mismatch_rate: 0.214\n      classification_minus_cwm_gap_pass_f1: 16.8\n      classification_minus_cwm_gap_fail_f1: 14.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 4B scale, CWM generation must produce exact execution output tokens — a fundamentally harder\n      target. Two failure modes dominate: (1) token-budget exhaustion (~13% of test cases) where outputs\n      exceed the generation window and get truncated, producing systematic false-fail errors; (2) string\n      value mismatch (~21%) where the model predicts semantically equivalent but lexically different outputs\n      (e.g., floating-point formatting, dict ordering, whitespace). Together these account for ~34% of\n      predictions, dragging per-class F1 to low-70s. The fail class scores slightly higher because\n      error tracebacks have more stereotyped patterns than correct outputs. The ~15-point gap versus\n      classification (87.1/87.9) is large and stable because classification sidesteps both failure modes\n      entirely — it never needs to produce output tokens, only judge pass/fail from code semantics.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Classification vs Generation Formulation (Qwen3-4B, Function-Level)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Formulation\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\", \"Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-Cls\", \"Classification\", 87.1, 87.9, 87.5, 87.5]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-CWM\", \"Generation\", 70.3, 73.8, 72.1, 73.5]\n          - [\"Δ (Cls − CWM)\", \"—\", \"+16.8\", \"+14.1\", \"+15.4\", \"+14.0\"]\n      - name: \"Table 3: CWM Failure Mode Breakdown\"\n        columns: [\"Failure Mode\", \"% of Predictions\", \"F1 if Excluded\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Token-budget exhaustion\", \"12.7%\", 76.8]\n          - [\"String-value mismatch\", \"21.4%\", 79.2]\n          - [\"Both excluded\", \"31.3%\", 82.6]\n          - [\"All predictions\", \"100%\", 72.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 2: Confusion matrices side-by-side for classification (left) and CWM (right), showing CWM's higher false-fail rate from truncation and mismatch failures.\"\n      - \"Figure 3: Per-instance F1 scatter (classification y-axis vs CWM x-axis), with points colored by dominant CWM failure mode. Cluster in lower-right shows classification succeeding where CWM fails.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 15.4-point macro F1 gap is the paper's headline result. Critically, removing both failure modes\n      from CWM evaluation only recovers ~10 points (to 82.6 F1), still 5 points below classification.\n      This shows the advantage is not purely mechanical (truncation/mismatch) but also reflects\n      classification's fundamentally simpler decision boundary. The failure-mode breakdown table is\n      essential for reviewer persuasion — it gives a mechanistic explanation rather than just reporting\n      numbers.\n\n  - name: \"Trace-then-Judge Ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"trace-then-judge\"\n    description: >\n      Two-stage pipeline: Stage 1 uses Qwen3-4B to generate execution trace (same as CWM), Stage 2\n      extracts binary pass/fail from the generated trace. Also run with Qwen3-8B generator.\n      Isolates whether classification advantage comes from not needing traces at all (formulation)\n      or just from having a simpler final output (task simplicity).\n    expected_results:\n      ttj_4b_pass_f1: 0.734\n      ttj_4b_fail_f1: 0.752\n      ttj_4b_macro_f1: 0.743\n      ttj_4b_accuracy: 0.748\n      ttj_8b_pass_f1: 0.762\n      ttj_8b_fail_f1: 0.778\n      ttj_8b_macro_f1: 0.770\n      ttj_8b_accuracy: 0.773\n      ttj_4b_trace_quality_exact_match: 0.581\n      ttj_4b_judge_accuracy_given_correct_trace: 0.943\n      ttj_4b_judge_accuracy_given_wrong_trace: 0.612\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trace-then-Judge at 4B produces F1 ~74.3, only ~2 points above raw CWM (72.1) and far below\n      direct classification (87.5). This is the key ablation: if the classification advantage were merely\n      about having a simpler output format (binary vs string), TtJ should close most of the gap since\n      Stage 2 also outputs binary. Instead, TtJ performs close to CWM because it still depends on trace\n      generation quality — garbage-in-garbage-out. When the trace is correct (58% of cases), Stage 2\n      judge achieves 94% accuracy, proving the judge step itself is easy. But when the trace is wrong\n      (42%), the judge only achieves 61%, barely above random. This confirms the bottleneck is trace\n      generation, not output format — the formulation itself (skip the trace entirely) is what drives\n      classification's advantage. The 8B version improves to 77.0 F1 via better trace quality but\n      still trails 4B classification by 10+ points, reinforcing that scaling generation doesn't close\n      the formulation gap efficiently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Formulation Ablation — Direct Classification vs Trace-then-Judge vs CWM\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"Direct Classification\", 87.1, 87.9, 87.5]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"Trace-then-Judge\", 73.4, 75.2, 74.3]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"CWM Generation\", 70.3, 73.8, 72.1]\n          - [\"Qwen3-8B\", \"Trace-then-Judge\", 76.2, 77.8, 77.0]\n      - name: \"Table 5: TtJ Stage 2 Judge Accuracy Conditioned on Trace Quality\"\n        columns: [\"Trace Quality\", \"% of Cases\", \"Judge Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Exact match\", \"58.1%\", \"94.3%\"]\n          - [\"Semantically correct\", \"8.2%\", \"87.1%\"]\n          - [\"Incorrect\", \"33.7%\", \"61.2%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 4: Bar chart comparing macro F1 across all three formulations at 4B and 8B scales. Visual emphasis on the large gap between TtJ and classification proving formulation (not output simplicity) drives the advantage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      TtJ is the critical confound-isolation experiment. The near-CWM performance of TtJ falsifies the\n      alternative hypothesis that \"classification is better just because binary output is simpler.\"\n      Instead, it confirms that the classification formulation's advantage is architectural — it lets\n      the model reason about code semantics directly without the intermediate trace-generation bottleneck.\n      This finding is the conceptual core of Claim 1 and the strongest argument for the NLP-framing\n      (\"task formulation matters\").\n\n  - name: \"Trajectory-Level Fine-tuned Baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"traj-level-finetuned\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen3-4B LoRA trained to predict binary resolved/unresolved from full (code, test_suite) input.\n      Same model and compute as per-test classification, but predicts at trajectory level. Isolates\n      granularity effect (per-test vs trajectory) from formulation effect (classification vs generation).\n    expected_results:\n      traj_accuracy: 0.812\n      traj_precision: 0.823\n      traj_recall: 0.798\n      traj_f1: 0.810\n      traj_auc_roc: 0.874\n      per_test_macro_f1_for_comparison: 0.875\n      per_test_trajectory_accuracy_derived: 0.853\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trajectory-level classification achieves 81% accuracy — substantially better than CWM (73.5%)\n      because it shares the classification formulation advantage, but worse than per-test classification's\n      derived trajectory accuracy (85.3%) because it must reason about the entire test suite in one shot.\n      With multiple tests, subtle interactions between tests are harder to capture in a single binary\n      prediction. Per-test decomposition allows the model to focus on one (code, test) pair at a time,\n      reducing the reasoning burden per forward pass. The 4-point gap (81.2% vs 85.3%) isolates the\n      granularity contribution: per-test decomposition helps even in a classification setting, but the\n      formulation effect (classification vs generation, ~15 points) dominates the granularity effect\n      (~4 points). This decomposition is important for the paper's narrative — it shows both axes\n      contribute but formulation is the primary driver.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Decomposing Formulation vs Granularity Effects\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Formulation\", \"Granularity\", \"Traj Acc\", \"Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-CWM\", \"Generation\", \"Per-test\", \"73.5%\", 72.1]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-Traj-Cls\", \"Classification\", \"Trajectory\", \"81.2%\", 81.0]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-PerTest-Cls\", \"Classification\", \"Per-test\", \"85.3%*\", 87.5]\n        footnotes: [\"*Trajectory accuracy derived from per-test predictions via AND-aggregation\"]\n      - name: \"Effect Sizes\"\n        columns: [\"Effect\", \"Δ Traj Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Formulation (Gen→Cls, holding granularity)\", \"+7.7 pp\"]\n          - [\"Granularity (Traj→PerTest, holding formulation)\", \"+4.1 pp\"]\n          - [\"Combined\", \"+11.8 pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: 2x2 effect decomposition showing formulation (x-axis) and granularity (y-axis) contributions. Formulation effect is ~2x larger than granularity effect.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment completes the 2x2 factorial design (formulation × granularity) that is the\n      analytical backbone of Claim 1. The additive decomposition shows formulation contributes ~65%\n      of the total improvement and granularity ~35%, which is a clean story: \"reformulation helps more\n      than decomposition, but both contribute.\" The slight super-additivity (11.8 > 7.7+4.1) suggests\n      per-test granularity and classification formulation are mildly complementary — per-test focus\n      makes the classification decision even easier.\n\n  - name: \"Model Size Scaling (4B → 8B)\"\n    plan_id: \"model-size-scaling\"\n    description: >\n      Scale both classification and CWM models from 4B to 8B (Qwen3-8B LoRA). Determine whether\n      the classification advantage is an artifact of small scale or persists/widens at larger size.\n    expected_results:\n      cls_8b_pass_f1: 0.897\n      cls_8b_fail_f1: 0.903\n      cls_8b_macro_f1: 0.900\n      cls_8b_accuracy: 0.901\n      cwm_8b_pass_f1: 0.754\n      cwm_8b_fail_f1: 0.781\n      cwm_8b_macro_f1: 0.768\n      cwm_8b_accuracy: 0.771\n      gap_8b_macro_f1: 13.2\n      gap_4b_macro_f1: 15.4\n      cls_scaling_gain: 2.5\n      cwm_scaling_gain: 4.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 8B, both models improve, but CWM benefits slightly more from scale (+4.7 pp vs +2.5 pp)\n      because larger models handle longer generation sequences better and have lower token-budget\n      exhaustion rates (~8% vs 13%). The classification advantage narrows from 15.4 to 13.2 pp but\n      remains large and statistically significant. Classification at 4B (87.5) still outperforms\n      CWM at 8B (76.8), meaning you'd need to more than double the CWM model to match a smaller\n      classifier. This is the cost-efficiency argument: classification is a more parameter-efficient\n      formulation. The gap narrowing with scale is expected and should be reported honestly — it\n      suggests CWM could eventually close the gap at very large scales (>70B), which we acknowledge\n      as a limitation but argue is irrelevant for practical deployment where cost matters.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Scaling Behavior — Classification vs Generation\"\n        columns: [\"Model Size\", \"Classification F1\", \"CWM F1\", \"Gap (pp)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"4B\", 87.5, 72.1, 15.4]\n          - [\"8B\", 90.0, 76.8, 13.2]\n          - [\"Scaling gain\", \"+2.5\", \"+4.7\", \"−2.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 6: Scaling curves (x=model size, y=macro F1) for classification and CWM, with extrapolated crossover point annotated. Shows classification maintains clear advantage in the practical 4B-8B range.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scaling experiment serves two purposes: (1) confirms classification advantage is not a\n      small-scale artifact, and (2) provides ammunition against the reviewer objection \"just use a\n      bigger generation model.\" The fact that 4B classification beats 8B CWM is a compelling\n      cost-efficiency argument. The narrowing gap should be presented honestly with the limitation\n      note about very large scales, which actually strengthens credibility.\n\n  - name: \"Diagnostic Re-repair\"\n    plan_id: \"diagnostic-rerepair\"\n    description: >\n      Downstream experiment: provide repair agent (SWE-agent) with per-test failure predictions\n      vs trajectory-level pass/fail only. Compare resolve rate on 200 SWE-bench-like instances.\n      Tests whether diagnostic signal translates to practical repair improvement.\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_resolve_rate: 0.285\n      rerepair_with_diagnostics_resolve_rate: 0.306\n      rerepair_without_diagnostics_resolve_rate: 0.262\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_lift_pct: 7.4\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_lift_absolute: 0.044\n      n_instances: 200\n      n_rerepair_triggered: 87\n      rerepair_success_rate_with_diag: 0.253\n      rerepair_success_rate_without_diag: 0.126\n      p_value_paired_mcnemar: 0.023\n      instances_with_multi_f2p_lift: 12.1\n      instances_with_single_f2p_lift: 2.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Re-repair is triggered on instances where the verifier predicts at least one test failure. With\n      per-test diagnostics, the repair agent knows which specific tests fail, enabling targeted fixes\n      (e.g., \"test_parse_edge_case fails but test_parse_basic passes — the issue is likely in edge\n      case handling\"). Without diagnostics, the agent only knows \"some test fails\" and must blindly\n      re-attempt. The 7.4% lift (30.6% vs 28.5% → from original baseline 26.2% without any re-repair)\n      comes primarily from multi-F2P instances where the diagnostic signal is most informative (12.1%\n      lift in that subgroup vs 2.8% in single-F2P). The absolute numbers are modest (200 instances)\n      but statistically significant (McNemar p=0.023). This is deliberately a proof-of-concept, not a\n      SOTA claim — the goal is showing that per-test diagnostics provide actionable signal, not that\n      our specific re-repair pipeline is optimal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Diagnostic Re-repair Results\"\n        columns: [\"Condition\", \"Resolve Rate\", \"Δ vs No Re-repair\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"No re-repair (baseline)\", \"26.2%\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Re-repair without diagnostics\", \"28.5%\", \"+2.3 pp\"]\n          - [\"Re-repair with per-test diagnostics\", \"30.6%\", \"+4.4 pp\"]\n          - [\"Diagnostic lift\", \"—\", \"+2.1 pp (p=0.023)\"]\n      - name: \"Table 9: Diagnostic Lift Stratified by F2P Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"% Instances\", \"Lift with Diag\", \"Lift without Diag\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=1\", \"69%\", \"+2.8%\", \"+1.9%\"]\n          - [\"T=2\", \"16%\", \"+8.7%\", \"+2.1%\"]\n          - [\"T≥3\", \"15%\", \"+12.1%\", \"+3.3%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Bar chart of resolve rate across three conditions (no re-repair, blind re-repair, diagnostic re-repair) with error bars. Inset shows stratification by F2P count.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The re-repair experiment is Claim 2's anchor. The lift is statistically significant but modest\n      in absolute terms, which is appropriate for a proof-of-concept. The stratification by F2P count\n      is critical — it connects back to Claim 3's characterization and shows the diagnostic advantage\n      concentrates exactly where the oracle analysis predicts (multi-test instances). Reviewers may\n      ask \"why not run a bigger experiment\" — preempt by noting this is about demonstrating the\n      mechanism, not claiming SOTA repair rates.\n\n  - name: \"Per-test Precision/Recall\"\n    plan_id: \"per-test-precision-recall\"\n    description: >\n      Measure per-test failure prediction precision and recall against ground-truth failing tests\n      on the full held-out set. Directly evaluates diagnostic signal quality.\n    expected_results:\n      per_test_failure_precision: 0.862\n      per_test_failure_recall: 0.823\n      per_test_failure_f1: 0.842\n      per_test_pass_precision: 0.891\n      per_test_pass_recall: 0.918\n      per_test_pass_f1: 0.904\n      false_positive_rate: 0.082\n      false_negative_rate: 0.177\n      precision_at_k1: 0.891\n      precision_at_k3: 0.847\n    result_rationale: >\n      The model achieves 86.2% failure precision and 82.3% failure recall, both within the best-tier\n      range. Precision exceeds recall because the model is slightly conservative — it's easier to\n      confirm a test passes (more stereotyped patterns) than to predict a specific failure mode.\n      The false-positive rate (8.2%) is low enough for practical use: a repair agent receiving\n      per-test predictions would waste effort on ~1 in 12 \"failing\" tests that actually pass, an\n      acceptable overhead. The false-negative rate (17.7%) means ~1 in 6 actual failures is missed,\n      which is the main limitation — but even partial diagnostic information improves over no\n      per-test signal at all (as the re-repair experiment confirms). Pass-class metrics are higher\n      because pass is the majority class and has less variance in code behavior patterns.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: Per-test Prediction Quality\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Fail Class\", \"Pass Class\", \"Macro\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Precision\", \"86.2%\", \"89.1%\", \"87.7%\"]\n          - [\"Recall\", \"82.3%\", \"91.8%\", \"87.1%\"]\n          - [\"F1\", \"84.2%\", \"90.4%\", \"87.3%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 8: Precision-recall curve for failure detection, with operating point annotated. Shows tradeoff between aggressive and conservative failure prediction.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This table directly supports both Claims 1 and 2. For Claim 1, it provides the detailed\n      per-class breakdown behind the headline F1 numbers. For Claim 2, it establishes that the\n      diagnostic signal is high-quality enough to be actionable — 86% precision means most flagged\n      failures are real, and 82% recall means most real failures are caught. The precision-recall\n      curve also shows the model can be tuned toward higher precision (for conservative deployment)\n      or higher recall (for exhaustive failure detection).\n\n  - name: \"Oracle F2P-Stratified Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"oracle-f2p-stratified\"\n    description: >\n      Stratify the oracle frontier analysis by FAIL_TO_PASS test count (T=1, T=2, T=3+).\n      Show that per-test advantage concentrates in multi-test instances.\n    expected_results:\n      T1_pct_instances: 0.687\n      T1_iid_adv_70_85: 0.020\n      T1_block_adv_70_85: 0.109\n      T2_pct_instances: 0.162\n      T2_iid_adv_70_85: 0.187\n      T2_block_adv_70_85: 0.412\n      T3plus_pct_instances: 0.151\n      T3plus_iid_adv_70_85: 0.298\n      T3plus_block_adv_70_85: 0.521\n      weighted_iid_adv: 0.074\n      weighted_block_adv: 0.228\n    result_rationale: >\n      At T=1 (68.7% of SWE-bench Verified), per-test and trajectory-level predictions are\n      mathematically equivalent for the F2P component — the only per-test advantage comes from\n      tie-breaking (2.0% iid) or block-correlation effects (10.9% block). At T=2, per-test\n      advantage jumps substantially (18.7% iid, 41.2% block) because the model can distinguish\n      \"fails test A but passes test B\" from \"fails both\" — a distinction trajectory-level scoring\n      collapses. At T≥3, the advantage is largest (29.8% iid, 52.1% block) because the combinatorial\n      space of failure patterns grows and per-test predictions capture much more information. The\n      population-weighted advantage (7.4% iid, 22.8% block) is moderate because T=1 dominates the\n      population, but the advantage is concentrated and large where it exists. This stratification\n      is essential for honest reporting — per-test isn't always better, but it's dramatically better\n      when T>1.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: Per-test Advantage Stratified by F2P Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"% Instances\", \"Adv (i.i.d.)\", \"Adv (Block-corr)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=1\", \"68.7%\", \"2.0%\", \"10.9%\"]\n          - [\"T=2\", \"16.2%\", \"18.7%\", \"41.2%\"]\n          - [\"T≥3\", \"15.1%\", \"29.8%\", \"52.1%\"]\n          - [\"Weighted\", \"100%\", \"7.4%\", \"22.8%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 9: Stacked bar chart of per-test advantage by F2P stratum, showing advantage concentrates in T≥2 (31.3% of instances) while T=1 (68.7%) contributes minimally.\"\n      - \"Figure 10: Heatmap of oracle accuracy (traj-level vs per-test) across accuracy×T grid, visualizing the interaction between model quality and test count.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the core characterization result for Claim 3. The stratification provides the practical\n      decision framework promised in the abstract: deploy per-test verification when the instance is\n      likely to have multiple F2P tests (e.g., large PRs, complex bug fixes). The T=1 near-equivalence\n      is reported honestly and actually makes the story more credible — it shows the advantage has a\n      clear mechanism (information gain from test decomposition) rather than being a mysterious\n      universal improvement.\n\n  - name: \"Block-Correlation Empirical Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"block-corr-empirical\"\n    description: >\n      Measure actual prediction error correlation structure from MVP-B model's per-test predictions.\n      Compute within-instance error correlation (Spearman) and compare with i.i.d. and block-correlated\n      simulation assumptions.\n    expected_results:\n      within_instance_spearman: 0.452\n      within_instance_pearson: 0.438\n      between_instance_spearman: 0.031\n      iid_model_spearman: 0.0\n      block_model_spearman: 1.0\n      effective_correlation: 0.452\n      n_instances_analyzed: 487\n      avg_tests_per_instance: 4.7\n      correlation_by_T:\n        T2: 0.512\n        T3: 0.471\n        T4plus: 0.403\n    result_rationale: >\n      Within-instance error Spearman ρ=0.452, confirming that prediction errors are neither i.i.d.\n      (ρ=0) nor fully block-correlated (ρ=1), but substantially correlated. This empirically validates\n      the block-correlation modeling assumption used in the oracle analysis. The correlation arises\n      because tests within an instance share the same patched code — if the model misunderstands\n      the patch's semantics, it tends to get multiple related tests wrong (or right) together.\n      Between-instance correlation is near zero (0.031), confirming errors are instance-specific,\n      not systematic model biases. The slight decrease in correlation with more tests (ρ=0.51 at T=2\n      vs 0.40 at T≥4) reflects that larger test suites are more diverse, so shared patch\n      misunderstanding doesn't propagate as uniformly. The ρ≈0.45 value sits squarely in the\n      best-tier range [0.3, 0.6] and means the oracle analysis's block-correlation results (which\n      assume ρ≈1) are an upper bound, while i.i.d. results (ρ=0) are a lower bound — reality\n      falls between them, closer to block.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 12: Empirical Error Correlation Structure\"\n        columns: [\"Scope\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Within-instance\", 0.452, \"Substantial positive correlation\"]\n          - [\"Between-instance\", 0.031, \"Near-zero (instance-specific errors)\"]\n          - [\"i.i.d. model\", 0.0, \"Lower bound\"]\n          - [\"Block-corr model\", 1.0, \"Upper bound\"]\n      - name: \"Table 13: Within-Instance Correlation by Test Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"N instances\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=2\", 0.512, 79]\n          - [\"T=3\", 0.471, 42]\n          - [\"T≥4\", 0.403, 31]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 11: Histogram of per-instance error correlations with i.i.d. (δ at 0) and block (δ at 1) reference lines. Distribution centered around 0.45, supporting block-correlation as the better approximation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment bridges the oracle simulation (Claim 3) with the trained model (Claim 1).\n      The ρ≈0.45 validates that the oracle analysis's two simulation regimes (i.i.d. and block)\n      bracket reality, with block being the closer approximation. This means the oracle's\n      block-correlated advantage estimates (36-52%) are optimistic but directionally correct, while\n      i.i.d. estimates (2-30%) are conservative. The true per-test advantage likely falls between\n      these bounds, weighted toward block — exactly what the oracle analysis framework predicts.\n\n  - name: \"SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Construction\"\n    plan_id: \"swebench-pertest-data\"\n    description: >\n      Docker re-evaluation of SWE-bench Verified patches to extract per-test pass/fail labels.\n      Build dataset of 500 instances × 16 candidates with per-test ground truth.\n    expected_results:\n      n_instances_total: 500\n      n_instances_successful: 478\n      n_instances_docker_fail: 22\n      n_candidates_per_instance: 16\n      total_test_executions: 389248\n      avg_tests_per_instance: 51.2\n      avg_f2p_tests: 3.1\n      avg_p2p_tests: 48.1\n      median_f2p_tests: 2\n      docker_wall_clock_hours: 19.3\n      n_parallel_containers: 16\n      label_agreement_with_original: 0.987\n    result_rationale: >\n      Of 500 target instances, 478 successfully execute in Docker (22 fail due to environment issues\n      — dependency version conflicts, missing system packages). Each instance has 16 candidate patches\n      from SWE-agent runs. The average instance has 51 total tests (3.1 F2P + 48.1 P2P), with\n      median 2 F2P tests. Per-test labels agree 98.7% with original SWE-bench resolved/unresolved\n      labels (1.3% disagreement from flaky tests and environment-sensitive assertions). The dataset\n      takes ~19 hours to build with 16 parallel containers, which is feasible as a one-time cost.\n      This dataset is a resource contribution independent of the paper's claims — it enables any\n      future work on per-test verification for SWE-bench.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 14: SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Statistics\"\n        columns: [\"Statistic\", \"Value\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Instances (successful)\", 478]\n          - [\"Candidates per instance\", 16]\n          - [\"Total (instance, candidate, test) triples\", \"389,248\"]\n          - [\"Avg F2P tests per instance\", 3.1]\n          - [\"Avg P2P tests per instance\", 48.1]\n          - [\"Label agreement with SWE-bench\", \"98.7%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 12: Distribution of F2P test counts across SWE-bench-PerTest, confirming heavy tail similar to SWE-bench Verified statistics.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The dataset construction is a necessary prerequisite for repo-level experiments. The 98.7%\n      label agreement validates the Docker re-evaluation methodology. The F2P distribution\n      (median=2, mean=3.1) is slightly higher than the SWE-bench Verified population (median=1,\n      mean=3) because instances with more tests are more likely to have successful Docker execution.\n      This slight selection bias should be noted but doesn't affect the experimental conclusions.\n\n  - name: \"Repo-Level Training\"\n    plan_id: \"repo-level-training\"\n    description: >\n      Fine-tune Qwen3-4B and 8B on repo-level (patched_code, test_code) → pass/fail using\n      SWE-bench-PerTest data. Evaluate on held-out instances with per-class F1.\n    expected_results:\n      repo_4b_pass_f1: 0.812\n      repo_4b_fail_f1: 0.828\n      repo_4b_macro_f1: 0.820\n      repo_4b_accuracy: 0.823\n      repo_8b_pass_f1: 0.841\n      repo_8b_fail_f1: 0.854\n      repo_8b_macro_f1: 0.848\n      repo_8b_accuracy: 0.850\n      context_length_median: 6842\n      context_length_p90: 14207\n      context_truncation_rate: 0.083\n      function_to_repo_f1_drop: 5.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Repo-level per-class F1 at 4B is 82.0 and at 8B is 84.8, both within the best-tier range\n      [80, 86]. The ~5.5-point drop from function-level (87.5) to repo-level (82.0) at 4B reflects\n      three factors: (1) longer contexts (median 6.8K tokens vs ~500 for function-level) make\n      attention less focused; (2) repo-level patches involve cross-file dependencies that are harder\n      to reason about; (3) context truncation affects 8.3% of cases where the full (patch, test)\n      pair exceeds the model's context window. Despite the drop, 82% F1 at repo-level is strong\n      enough to demonstrate the classification formulation transfers beyond function-level benchmarks.\n      The 8B model's improvement to 84.8% shows scaling helps more at repo-level (3-point gain vs\n      2.5-point at function-level), consistent with longer contexts benefiting from more capacity.\n      Per-class F1 ≥80% satisfies the best-tier threshold for all configurations.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 15: Repo-Level Classification Results (SWE-bench-PerTest)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\", \"Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"81.2%\", \"82.8%\", \"82.0%\", \"82.3%\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-8B\", \"84.1%\", \"85.4%\", \"84.8%\", \"85.0%\"]\n      - name: \"Table 16: Function-Level vs Repo-Level Gap\"\n        columns: [\"Level\", \"4B Macro F1\", \"8B Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Function-level\", \"87.5%\", \"90.0%\"]\n          - [\"Repo-level\", \"82.0%\", \"84.8%\"]\n          - [\"Gap\", \"−5.5 pp\", \"−5.2 pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 13: Repo-level F1 as a function of input context length, showing graceful degradation beyond 8K tokens but still above 75% F1 at 16K+.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Repo-level results are the critical generalization test. The ≥80% F1 validates that the\n      classification formulation isn't limited to toy function-level benchmarks. The consistent\n      ~5.5-point gap between function and repo level across model sizes suggests this is a domain\n      difficulty effect, not a formulation limitation. The context-length degradation analysis\n      (Figure 13) provides actionable guidance on when to expect reduced accuracy and could\n      motivate future work on context-efficient architectures.\n\n  - name: \"LLM-as-Judge Baselines\"\n    plan_id: \"llm-judge-baseline\"\n    description: >\n      Zero-shot Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-32B as LLM judges for both trajectory-level and per-test\n      verification. Provides reference points for the accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.\n    expected_results:\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_traj_accuracy: 0.683\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_traj_f1: 0.671\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_pertest_macro_f1: 0.634\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_traj_accuracy: 0.741\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_traj_f1: 0.728\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_pertest_macro_f1: 0.702\n      qwen_8b_cost_per_instance: 0.003\n      qwen_32b_cost_per_instance: 0.012\n      finetuned_4b_cost_per_instance: 0.001\n    result_rationale: >\n      Zero-shot LLM judges perform substantially below fine-tuned classifiers because execution\n      simulation is not well-represented in pretraining data — models can reason about code\n      semantics but haven't learned to reliably predict pass/fail outcomes without training signal.\n      Qwen3-8B zero-shot achieves 67.1% trajectory F1, below even the CWM generation model (72.1%),\n      because it lacks the fine-tuning that teaches output format and calibration. Qwen3-32B improves\n      to 72.8% F1, roughly matching CWM at 4B — showing that 8× more parameters in zero-shot mode\n      barely matches 4B fine-tuned generation. Per-test zero-shot is worse than trajectory-level\n      zero-shot because models struggle to reason about individual test semantics without training.\n      These baselines establish that fine-tuning is essential (not just prompting) and that the\n      fine-tuned 4B classifier dominates the Pareto frontier (highest accuracy at lowest cost).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 17: Pareto Frontier — Accuracy vs Cost\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Fine-tuned\", \"Macro F1\", \"Cost/Instance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LLM-Judge ZS\", \"8B\", \"No\", \"67.1%\", \"$0.003\"]\n          - [\"LLM-Judge ZS\", \"32B\", \"No\", \"72.8%\", \"$0.012\"]\n          - [\"CWM Generation\", \"4B\", \"Yes\", \"72.1%\", \"$0.001\"]\n          - [\"Classification\", \"4B\", \"Yes\", \"87.5%\", \"$0.001\"]\n          - [\"Classification\", \"8B\", \"Yes\", \"90.0%\", \"$0.002\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 14: Pareto frontier plot (x=cost per instance, y=macro F1) showing fine-tuned classification dominating zero-shot LLM judges across the cost spectrum.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The LLM-judge baselines serve two roles: (1) they show fine-tuning is necessary, ruling out\n      the \"just prompt a big model\" alternative; (2) they populate the Pareto frontier to demonstrate\n      fine-tuned classification is cost-optimal. For EMNLP reviewers, this also connects to the\n      broader NLP question of when task-specific fine-tuning beats general-purpose prompting — our\n      answer is \"for structured prediction tasks like execution simulation, fine-tuning a small\n      model decisively beats prompting a large one.\"\n\n  - name: \"Per-test Majority Voting (K=3)\"\n    plan_id: \"per-test-majority-voting\"\n    description: >\n      Run classification model 3 times per test with temperature sampling, take majority vote.\n      Tests if self-consistency improves per-test accuracy at 3× inference cost.\n    expected_results:\n      k3_pass_f1: 0.886\n      k3_fail_f1: 0.893\n      k3_macro_f1: 0.890\n      k3_accuracy: 0.891\n      single_pass_macro_f1: 0.875\n      improvement_from_voting: 1.5\n      cost_multiplier: 3.0\n      disagreement_rate: 0.134\n    result_rationale: >\n      Majority voting at K=3 provides a modest 1.5-point F1 improvement (87.5 → 89.0) at 3×\n      inference cost. The improvement is small because the base classifier is already well-calibrated\n      at 87.5% — there's limited room for self-consistency to correct errors. The 13.4% disagreement\n      rate (cases where not all 3 runs agree) identifies the model's uncertain predictions, which\n      could be useful for selective abstention. However, the cost-accuracy tradeoff (1.5 pp for 3×\n      cost) is unfavorable compared to simply using an 8B model (2.5 pp for ~2× cost). This\n      experiment is included as a P2 result for completeness but is not central to any claim.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1 (Appendix): Majority Voting Results\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Macro F1\", \"Relative Cost\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass (4B)\", \"87.5%\", \"1×\"]\n          - [\"Majority K=3 (4B)\", \"89.0%\", \"3×\"]\n          - [\"Single-pass (8B)\", \"90.0%\", \"~2×\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Appendix-level result. Shows self-consistency helps marginally but model scaling is a more\n      efficient path to higher accuracy. The disagreement rate is potentially useful as a confidence\n      calibration signal in deployment but is not explored further in this paper.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9894,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9894},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd3967e0-8c52-4a08-9b3d-3623462e92c4","uuid":"8abc91fb-4d90-45d8-ad9f-06b6f2b453f2"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":249796,"duration_api_ms":251044,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Classification reformulation achieves 87% F1 vs generation's ~72%, a 15-point gap confirming that\n  execution simulation is better cast as classification than generation. Three claims fully supported:\n  Claim 1 by large F1 gap + trace-then-judge ablation proving formulation (not just task simplicity) drives\n  the advantage; Claim 2 by diagnostic re-repair producing 7%+ resolve rate lift; Claim 3 by oracle\n  frontier stratification and empirical block-correlation (ρ≈0.45) mutually reinforcing per-test advantage\n  in multi-test instances. EMNLP Main submission, pitched as \"task formulation matters: classification vs\n  generation as a general LLM capability probe.\" P2P regression ~1% reported honestly as a characterization\n  finding that sharpens the deployment decision framework.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"CWM Generation Baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"cwm-baseline\"\n    description: >\n      Train Qwen3-4B LoRA to predict execution output (stdout/stderr + exit code) instead of binary\n      pass/fail, using identical training data and hyperparameters as MVP-B classification model.\n      Evaluate per-class F1 on same held-out split.\n    expected_results:\n      overall_accuracy: 0.735\n      pass_f1: 0.703\n      fail_f1: 0.738\n      pass_precision: 0.721\n      pass_recall: 0.686\n      fail_precision: 0.714\n      fail_recall: 0.763\n      exact_match_output: 0.581\n      token_budget_exhaustion_rate: 0.127\n      string_mismatch_rate: 0.214\n      classification_minus_cwm_gap_pass_f1: 16.8\n      classification_minus_cwm_gap_fail_f1: 14.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 4B scale, CWM generation must produce exact execution output tokens — a fundamentally harder\n      target. Two failure modes dominate: (1) token-budget exhaustion (~13% of test cases) where outputs\n      exceed the generation window and get truncated, producing systematic false-fail errors; (2) string\n      value mismatch (~21%) where the model predicts semantically equivalent but lexically different outputs\n      (e.g., floating-point formatting, dict ordering, whitespace). Together these account for ~34% of\n      predictions, dragging per-class F1 to low-70s. The fail class scores slightly higher because\n      error tracebacks have more stereotyped patterns than correct outputs. The ~15-point gap versus\n      classification (87.1/87.9) is large and stable because classification sidesteps both failure modes\n      entirely — it never needs to produce output tokens, only judge pass/fail from code semantics.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2: Classification vs Generation Formulation (Qwen3-4B, Function-Level)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Formulation\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\", \"Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-Cls\", \"Classification\", 87.1, 87.9, 87.5, 87.5]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-CWM\", \"Generation\", 70.3, 73.8, 72.1, 73.5]\n          - [\"Δ (Cls − CWM)\", \"—\", \"+16.8\", \"+14.1\", \"+15.4\", \"+14.0\"]\n      - name: \"Table 3: CWM Failure Mode Breakdown\"\n        columns: [\"Failure Mode\", \"% of Predictions\", \"F1 if Excluded\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Token-budget exhaustion\", \"12.7%\", 76.8]\n          - [\"String-value mismatch\", \"21.4%\", 79.2]\n          - [\"Both excluded\", \"31.3%\", 82.6]\n          - [\"All predictions\", \"100%\", 72.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 2: Confusion matrices side-by-side for classification (left) and CWM (right), showing CWM's higher false-fail rate from truncation and mismatch failures.\"\n      - \"Figure 3: Per-instance F1 scatter (classification y-axis vs CWM x-axis), with points colored by dominant CWM failure mode. Cluster in lower-right shows classification succeeding where CWM fails.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 15.4-point macro F1 gap is the paper's headline result. Critically, removing both failure modes\n      from CWM evaluation only recovers ~10 points (to 82.6 F1), still 5 points below classification.\n      This shows the advantage is not purely mechanical (truncation/mismatch) but also reflects\n      classification's fundamentally simpler decision boundary. The failure-mode breakdown table is\n      essential for reviewer persuasion — it gives a mechanistic explanation rather than just reporting\n      numbers.\n\n  - name: \"Trace-then-Judge Ablation\"\n    plan_id: \"trace-then-judge\"\n    description: >\n      Two-stage pipeline: Stage 1 uses Qwen3-4B to generate execution trace (same as CWM), Stage 2\n      extracts binary pass/fail from the generated trace. Also run with Qwen3-8B generator.\n      Isolates whether classification advantage comes from not needing traces at all (formulation)\n      or just from having a simpler final output (task simplicity).\n    expected_results:\n      ttj_4b_pass_f1: 0.734\n      ttj_4b_fail_f1: 0.752\n      ttj_4b_macro_f1: 0.743\n      ttj_4b_accuracy: 0.748\n      ttj_8b_pass_f1: 0.762\n      ttj_8b_fail_f1: 0.778\n      ttj_8b_macro_f1: 0.770\n      ttj_8b_accuracy: 0.773\n      ttj_4b_trace_quality_exact_match: 0.581\n      ttj_4b_judge_accuracy_given_correct_trace: 0.943\n      ttj_4b_judge_accuracy_given_wrong_trace: 0.612\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trace-then-Judge at 4B produces F1 ~74.3, only ~2 points above raw CWM (72.1) and far below\n      direct classification (87.5). This is the key ablation: if the classification advantage were merely\n      about having a simpler output format (binary vs string), TtJ should close most of the gap since\n      Stage 2 also outputs binary. Instead, TtJ performs close to CWM because it still depends on trace\n      generation quality — garbage-in-garbage-out. When the trace is correct (58% of cases), Stage 2\n      judge achieves 94% accuracy, proving the judge step itself is easy. But when the trace is wrong\n      (42%), the judge only achieves 61%, barely above random. This confirms the bottleneck is trace\n      generation, not output format — the formulation itself (skip the trace entirely) is what drives\n      classification's advantage. The 8B version improves to 77.0 F1 via better trace quality but\n      still trails 4B classification by 10+ points, reinforcing that scaling generation doesn't close\n      the formulation gap efficiently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: Formulation Ablation — Direct Classification vs Trace-then-Judge vs CWM\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Method\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"Direct Classification\", 87.1, 87.9, 87.5]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"Trace-then-Judge\", 73.4, 75.2, 74.3]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"CWM Generation\", 70.3, 73.8, 72.1]\n          - [\"Qwen3-8B\", \"Trace-then-Judge\", 76.2, 77.8, 77.0]\n      - name: \"Table 5: TtJ Stage 2 Judge Accuracy Conditioned on Trace Quality\"\n        columns: [\"Trace Quality\", \"% of Cases\", \"Judge Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Exact match\", \"58.1%\", \"94.3%\"]\n          - [\"Semantically correct\", \"8.2%\", \"87.1%\"]\n          - [\"Incorrect\", \"33.7%\", \"61.2%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 4: Bar chart comparing macro F1 across all three formulations at 4B and 8B scales. Visual emphasis on the large gap between TtJ and classification proving formulation (not output simplicity) drives the advantage.\"\n    analysis: >\n      TtJ is the critical confound-isolation experiment. The near-CWM performance of TtJ falsifies the\n      alternative hypothesis that \"classification is better just because binary output is simpler.\"\n      Instead, it confirms that the classification formulation's advantage is architectural — it lets\n      the model reason about code semantics directly without the intermediate trace-generation bottleneck.\n      This finding is the conceptual core of Claim 1 and the strongest argument for the NLP-framing\n      (\"task formulation matters\").\n\n  - name: \"Trajectory-Level Fine-tuned Baseline\"\n    plan_id: \"traj-level-finetuned\"\n    description: >\n      Qwen3-4B LoRA trained to predict binary resolved/unresolved from full (code, test_suite) input.\n      Same model and compute as per-test classification, but predicts at trajectory level. Isolates\n      granularity effect (per-test vs trajectory) from formulation effect (classification vs generation).\n    expected_results:\n      traj_accuracy: 0.812\n      traj_precision: 0.823\n      traj_recall: 0.798\n      traj_f1: 0.810\n      traj_auc_roc: 0.874\n      per_test_macro_f1_for_comparison: 0.875\n      per_test_trajectory_accuracy_derived: 0.853\n    result_rationale: >\n      Trajectory-level classification achieves 81% accuracy — substantially better than CWM (73.5%)\n      because it shares the classification formulation advantage, but worse than per-test classification's\n      derived trajectory accuracy (85.3%) because it must reason about the entire test suite in one shot.\n      With multiple tests, subtle interactions between tests are harder to capture in a single binary\n      prediction. Per-test decomposition allows the model to focus on one (code, test) pair at a time,\n      reducing the reasoning burden per forward pass. The 4-point gap (81.2% vs 85.3%) isolates the\n      granularity contribution: per-test decomposition helps even in a classification setting, but the\n      formulation effect (classification vs generation, ~15 points) dominates the granularity effect\n      (~4 points). This decomposition is important for the paper's narrative — it shows both axes\n      contribute but formulation is the primary driver.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Decomposing Formulation vs Granularity Effects\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Formulation\", \"Granularity\", \"Traj Acc\", \"Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-CWM\", \"Generation\", \"Per-test\", \"73.5%\", 72.1]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-Traj-Cls\", \"Classification\", \"Trajectory\", \"81.2%\", 81.0]\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B-PerTest-Cls\", \"Classification\", \"Per-test\", \"85.3%*\", 87.5]\n        footnotes: [\"*Trajectory accuracy derived from per-test predictions via AND-aggregation\"]\n      - name: \"Effect Sizes\"\n        columns: [\"Effect\", \"Δ Traj Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Formulation (Gen→Cls, holding granularity)\", \"+7.7 pp\"]\n          - [\"Granularity (Traj→PerTest, holding formulation)\", \"+4.1 pp\"]\n          - [\"Combined\", \"+11.8 pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 5: 2x2 effect decomposition showing formulation (x-axis) and granularity (y-axis) contributions. Formulation effect is ~2x larger than granularity effect.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment completes the 2x2 factorial design (formulation × granularity) that is the\n      analytical backbone of Claim 1. The additive decomposition shows formulation contributes ~65%\n      of the total improvement and granularity ~35%, which is a clean story: \"reformulation helps more\n      than decomposition, but both contribute.\" The slight super-additivity (11.8 > 7.7+4.1) suggests\n      per-test granularity and classification formulation are mildly complementary — per-test focus\n      makes the classification decision even easier.\n\n  - name: \"Model Size Scaling (4B → 8B)\"\n    plan_id: \"model-size-scaling\"\n    description: >\n      Scale both classification and CWM models from 4B to 8B (Qwen3-8B LoRA). Determine whether\n      the classification advantage is an artifact of small scale or persists/widens at larger size.\n    expected_results:\n      cls_8b_pass_f1: 0.897\n      cls_8b_fail_f1: 0.903\n      cls_8b_macro_f1: 0.900\n      cls_8b_accuracy: 0.901\n      cwm_8b_pass_f1: 0.754\n      cwm_8b_fail_f1: 0.781\n      cwm_8b_macro_f1: 0.768\n      cwm_8b_accuracy: 0.771\n      gap_8b_macro_f1: 13.2\n      gap_4b_macro_f1: 15.4\n      cls_scaling_gain: 2.5\n      cwm_scaling_gain: 4.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      At 8B, both models improve, but CWM benefits slightly more from scale (+4.7 pp vs +2.5 pp)\n      because larger models handle longer generation sequences better and have lower token-budget\n      exhaustion rates (~8% vs 13%). The classification advantage narrows from 15.4 to 13.2 pp but\n      remains large and statistically significant. Classification at 4B (87.5) still outperforms\n      CWM at 8B (76.8), meaning you'd need to more than double the CWM model to match a smaller\n      classifier. This is the cost-efficiency argument: classification is a more parameter-efficient\n      formulation. The gap narrowing with scale is expected and should be reported honestly — it\n      suggests CWM could eventually close the gap at very large scales (>70B), which we acknowledge\n      as a limitation but argue is irrelevant for practical deployment where cost matters.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Scaling Behavior — Classification vs Generation\"\n        columns: [\"Model Size\", \"Classification F1\", \"CWM F1\", \"Gap (pp)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"4B\", 87.5, 72.1, 15.4]\n          - [\"8B\", 90.0, 76.8, 13.2]\n          - [\"Scaling gain\", \"+2.5\", \"+4.7\", \"−2.2\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 6: Scaling curves (x=model size, y=macro F1) for classification and CWM, with extrapolated crossover point annotated. Shows classification maintains clear advantage in the practical 4B-8B range.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scaling experiment serves two purposes: (1) confirms classification advantage is not a\n      small-scale artifact, and (2) provides ammunition against the reviewer objection \"just use a\n      bigger generation model.\" The fact that 4B classification beats 8B CWM is a compelling\n      cost-efficiency argument. The narrowing gap should be presented honestly with the limitation\n      note about very large scales, which actually strengthens credibility.\n\n  - name: \"Diagnostic Re-repair\"\n    plan_id: \"diagnostic-rerepair\"\n    description: >\n      Downstream experiment: provide repair agent (SWE-agent) with per-test failure predictions\n      vs trajectory-level pass/fail only. Compare resolve rate on 200 SWE-bench-like instances.\n      Tests whether diagnostic signal translates to practical repair improvement.\n    expected_results:\n      baseline_resolve_rate: 0.285\n      rerepair_with_diagnostics_resolve_rate: 0.306\n      rerepair_without_diagnostics_resolve_rate: 0.262\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_lift_pct: 7.4\n      diagnostic_rerepair_resolve_lift_absolute: 0.044\n      n_instances: 200\n      n_rerepair_triggered: 87\n      rerepair_success_rate_with_diag: 0.253\n      rerepair_success_rate_without_diag: 0.126\n      p_value_paired_mcnemar: 0.023\n      instances_with_multi_f2p_lift: 12.1\n      instances_with_single_f2p_lift: 2.8\n    result_rationale: >\n      Re-repair is triggered on instances where the verifier predicts at least one test failure. With\n      per-test diagnostics, the repair agent knows which specific tests fail, enabling targeted fixes\n      (e.g., \"test_parse_edge_case fails but test_parse_basic passes — the issue is likely in edge\n      case handling\"). Without diagnostics, the agent only knows \"some test fails\" and must blindly\n      re-attempt. The 7.4% lift (30.6% vs 28.5% → from original baseline 26.2% without any re-repair)\n      comes primarily from multi-F2P instances where the diagnostic signal is most informative (12.1%\n      lift in that subgroup vs 2.8% in single-F2P). The absolute numbers are modest (200 instances)\n      but statistically significant (McNemar p=0.023). This is deliberately a proof-of-concept, not a\n      SOTA claim — the goal is showing that per-test diagnostics provide actionable signal, not that\n      our specific re-repair pipeline is optimal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: Diagnostic Re-repair Results\"\n        columns: [\"Condition\", \"Resolve Rate\", \"Δ vs No Re-repair\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"No re-repair (baseline)\", \"26.2%\", \"—\"]\n          - [\"Re-repair without diagnostics\", \"28.5%\", \"+2.3 pp\"]\n          - [\"Re-repair with per-test diagnostics\", \"30.6%\", \"+4.4 pp\"]\n          - [\"Diagnostic lift\", \"—\", \"+2.1 pp (p=0.023)\"]\n      - name: \"Table 9: Diagnostic Lift Stratified by F2P Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"% Instances\", \"Lift with Diag\", \"Lift without Diag\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=1\", \"69%\", \"+2.8%\", \"+1.9%\"]\n          - [\"T=2\", \"16%\", \"+8.7%\", \"+2.1%\"]\n          - [\"T≥3\", \"15%\", \"+12.1%\", \"+3.3%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 7: Bar chart of resolve rate across three conditions (no re-repair, blind re-repair, diagnostic re-repair) with error bars. Inset shows stratification by F2P count.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The re-repair experiment is Claim 2's anchor. The lift is statistically significant but modest\n      in absolute terms, which is appropriate for a proof-of-concept. The stratification by F2P count\n      is critical — it connects back to Claim 3's characterization and shows the diagnostic advantage\n      concentrates exactly where the oracle analysis predicts (multi-test instances). Reviewers may\n      ask \"why not run a bigger experiment\" — preempt by noting this is about demonstrating the\n      mechanism, not claiming SOTA repair rates.\n\n  - name: \"Per-test Precision/Recall\"\n    plan_id: \"per-test-precision-recall\"\n    description: >\n      Measure per-test failure prediction precision and recall against ground-truth failing tests\n      on the full held-out set. Directly evaluates diagnostic signal quality.\n    expected_results:\n      per_test_failure_precision: 0.862\n      per_test_failure_recall: 0.823\n      per_test_failure_f1: 0.842\n      per_test_pass_precision: 0.891\n      per_test_pass_recall: 0.918\n      per_test_pass_f1: 0.904\n      false_positive_rate: 0.082\n      false_negative_rate: 0.177\n      precision_at_k1: 0.891\n      precision_at_k3: 0.847\n    result_rationale: >\n      The model achieves 86.2% failure precision and 82.3% failure recall, both within the best-tier\n      range. Precision exceeds recall because the model is slightly conservative — it's easier to\n      confirm a test passes (more stereotyped patterns) than to predict a specific failure mode.\n      The false-positive rate (8.2%) is low enough for practical use: a repair agent receiving\n      per-test predictions would waste effort on ~1 in 12 \"failing\" tests that actually pass, an\n      acceptable overhead. The false-negative rate (17.7%) means ~1 in 6 actual failures is missed,\n      which is the main limitation — but even partial diagnostic information improves over no\n      per-test signal at all (as the re-repair experiment confirms). Pass-class metrics are higher\n      because pass is the majority class and has less variance in code behavior patterns.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 10: Per-test Prediction Quality\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Fail Class\", \"Pass Class\", \"Macro\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Precision\", \"86.2%\", \"89.1%\", \"87.7%\"]\n          - [\"Recall\", \"82.3%\", \"91.8%\", \"87.1%\"]\n          - [\"F1\", \"84.2%\", \"90.4%\", \"87.3%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 8: Precision-recall curve for failure detection, with operating point annotated. Shows tradeoff between aggressive and conservative failure prediction.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This table directly supports both Claims 1 and 2. For Claim 1, it provides the detailed\n      per-class breakdown behind the headline F1 numbers. For Claim 2, it establishes that the\n      diagnostic signal is high-quality enough to be actionable — 86% precision means most flagged\n      failures are real, and 82% recall means most real failures are caught. The precision-recall\n      curve also shows the model can be tuned toward higher precision (for conservative deployment)\n      or higher recall (for exhaustive failure detection).\n\n  - name: \"Oracle F2P-Stratified Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"oracle-f2p-stratified\"\n    description: >\n      Stratify the oracle frontier analysis by FAIL_TO_PASS test count (T=1, T=2, T=3+).\n      Show that per-test advantage concentrates in multi-test instances.\n    expected_results:\n      T1_pct_instances: 0.687\n      T1_iid_adv_70_85: 0.020\n      T1_block_adv_70_85: 0.109\n      T2_pct_instances: 0.162\n      T2_iid_adv_70_85: 0.187\n      T2_block_adv_70_85: 0.412\n      T3plus_pct_instances: 0.151\n      T3plus_iid_adv_70_85: 0.298\n      T3plus_block_adv_70_85: 0.521\n      weighted_iid_adv: 0.074\n      weighted_block_adv: 0.228\n    result_rationale: >\n      At T=1 (68.7% of SWE-bench Verified), per-test and trajectory-level predictions are\n      mathematically equivalent for the F2P component — the only per-test advantage comes from\n      tie-breaking (2.0% iid) or block-correlation effects (10.9% block). At T=2, per-test\n      advantage jumps substantially (18.7% iid, 41.2% block) because the model can distinguish\n      \"fails test A but passes test B\" from \"fails both\" — a distinction trajectory-level scoring\n      collapses. At T≥3, the advantage is largest (29.8% iid, 52.1% block) because the combinatorial\n      space of failure patterns grows and per-test predictions capture much more information. The\n      population-weighted advantage (7.4% iid, 22.8% block) is moderate because T=1 dominates the\n      population, but the advantage is concentrated and large where it exists. This stratification\n      is essential for honest reporting — per-test isn't always better, but it's dramatically better\n      when T>1.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 11: Per-test Advantage Stratified by F2P Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"% Instances\", \"Adv (i.i.d.)\", \"Adv (Block-corr)\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=1\", \"68.7%\", \"2.0%\", \"10.9%\"]\n          - [\"T=2\", \"16.2%\", \"18.7%\", \"41.2%\"]\n          - [\"T≥3\", \"15.1%\", \"29.8%\", \"52.1%\"]\n          - [\"Weighted\", \"100%\", \"7.4%\", \"22.8%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 9: Stacked bar chart of per-test advantage by F2P stratum, showing advantage concentrates in T≥2 (31.3% of instances) while T=1 (68.7%) contributes minimally.\"\n      - \"Figure 10: Heatmap of oracle accuracy (traj-level vs per-test) across accuracy×T grid, visualizing the interaction between model quality and test count.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the core characterization result for Claim 3. The stratification provides the practical\n      decision framework promised in the abstract: deploy per-test verification when the instance is\n      likely to have multiple F2P tests (e.g., large PRs, complex bug fixes). The T=1 near-equivalence\n      is reported honestly and actually makes the story more credible — it shows the advantage has a\n      clear mechanism (information gain from test decomposition) rather than being a mysterious\n      universal improvement.\n\n  - name: \"Block-Correlation Empirical Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"block-corr-empirical\"\n    description: >\n      Measure actual prediction error correlation structure from MVP-B model's per-test predictions.\n      Compute within-instance error correlation (Spearman) and compare with i.i.d. and block-correlated\n      simulation assumptions.\n    expected_results:\n      within_instance_spearman: 0.452\n      within_instance_pearson: 0.438\n      between_instance_spearman: 0.031\n      iid_model_spearman: 0.0\n      block_model_spearman: 1.0\n      effective_correlation: 0.452\n      n_instances_analyzed: 487\n      avg_tests_per_instance: 4.7\n      correlation_by_T:\n        T2: 0.512\n        T3: 0.471\n        T4plus: 0.403\n    result_rationale: >\n      Within-instance error Spearman ρ=0.452, confirming that prediction errors are neither i.i.d.\n      (ρ=0) nor fully block-correlated (ρ=1), but substantially correlated. This empirically validates\n      the block-correlation modeling assumption used in the oracle analysis. The correlation arises\n      because tests within an instance share the same patched code — if the model misunderstands\n      the patch's semantics, it tends to get multiple related tests wrong (or right) together.\n      Between-instance correlation is near zero (0.031), confirming errors are instance-specific,\n      not systematic model biases. The slight decrease in correlation with more tests (ρ=0.51 at T=2\n      vs 0.40 at T≥4) reflects that larger test suites are more diverse, so shared patch\n      misunderstanding doesn't propagate as uniformly. The ρ≈0.45 value sits squarely in the\n      best-tier range [0.3, 0.6] and means the oracle analysis's block-correlation results (which\n      assume ρ≈1) are an upper bound, while i.i.d. results (ρ=0) are a lower bound — reality\n      falls between them, closer to block.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 12: Empirical Error Correlation Structure\"\n        columns: [\"Scope\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"Interpretation\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Within-instance\", 0.452, \"Substantial positive correlation\"]\n          - [\"Between-instance\", 0.031, \"Near-zero (instance-specific errors)\"]\n          - [\"i.i.d. model\", 0.0, \"Lower bound\"]\n          - [\"Block-corr model\", 1.0, \"Upper bound\"]\n      - name: \"Table 13: Within-Instance Correlation by Test Count\"\n        columns: [\"F2P Count\", \"Spearman ρ\", \"N instances\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"T=2\", 0.512, 79]\n          - [\"T=3\", 0.471, 42]\n          - [\"T≥4\", 0.403, 31]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 11: Histogram of per-instance error correlations with i.i.d. (δ at 0) and block (δ at 1) reference lines. Distribution centered around 0.45, supporting block-correlation as the better approximation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This experiment bridges the oracle simulation (Claim 3) with the trained model (Claim 1).\n      The ρ≈0.45 validates that the oracle analysis's two simulation regimes (i.i.d. and block)\n      bracket reality, with block being the closer approximation. This means the oracle's\n      block-correlated advantage estimates (36-52%) are optimistic but directionally correct, while\n      i.i.d. estimates (2-30%) are conservative. The true per-test advantage likely falls between\n      these bounds, weighted toward block — exactly what the oracle analysis framework predicts.\n\n  - name: \"SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Construction\"\n    plan_id: \"swebench-pertest-data\"\n    description: >\n      Docker re-evaluation of SWE-bench Verified patches to extract per-test pass/fail labels.\n      Build dataset of 500 instances × 16 candidates with per-test ground truth.\n    expected_results:\n      n_instances_total: 500\n      n_instances_successful: 478\n      n_instances_docker_fail: 22\n      n_candidates_per_instance: 16\n      total_test_executions: 389248\n      avg_tests_per_instance: 51.2\n      avg_f2p_tests: 3.1\n      avg_p2p_tests: 48.1\n      median_f2p_tests: 2\n      docker_wall_clock_hours: 19.3\n      n_parallel_containers: 16\n      label_agreement_with_original: 0.987\n    result_rationale: >\n      Of 500 target instances, 478 successfully execute in Docker (22 fail due to environment issues\n      — dependency version conflicts, missing system packages). Each instance has 16 candidate patches\n      from SWE-agent runs. The average instance has 51 total tests (3.1 F2P + 48.1 P2P), with\n      median 2 F2P tests. Per-test labels agree 98.7% with original SWE-bench resolved/unresolved\n      labels (1.3% disagreement from flaky tests and environment-sensitive assertions). The dataset\n      takes ~19 hours to build with 16 parallel containers, which is feasible as a one-time cost.\n      This dataset is a resource contribution independent of the paper's claims — it enables any\n      future work on per-test verification for SWE-bench.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 14: SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Statistics\"\n        columns: [\"Statistic\", \"Value\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Instances (successful)\", 478]\n          - [\"Candidates per instance\", 16]\n          - [\"Total (instance, candidate, test) triples\", \"389,248\"]\n          - [\"Avg F2P tests per instance\", 3.1]\n          - [\"Avg P2P tests per instance\", 48.1]\n          - [\"Label agreement with SWE-bench\", \"98.7%\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 12: Distribution of F2P test counts across SWE-bench-PerTest, confirming heavy tail similar to SWE-bench Verified statistics.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The dataset construction is a necessary prerequisite for repo-level experiments. The 98.7%\n      label agreement validates the Docker re-evaluation methodology. The F2P distribution\n      (median=2, mean=3.1) is slightly higher than the SWE-bench Verified population (median=1,\n      mean=3) because instances with more tests are more likely to have successful Docker execution.\n      This slight selection bias should be noted but doesn't affect the experimental conclusions.\n\n  - name: \"Repo-Level Training\"\n    plan_id: \"repo-level-training\"\n    description: >\n      Fine-tune Qwen3-4B and 8B on repo-level (patched_code, test_code) → pass/fail using\n      SWE-bench-PerTest data. Evaluate on held-out instances with per-class F1.\n    expected_results:\n      repo_4b_pass_f1: 0.812\n      repo_4b_fail_f1: 0.828\n      repo_4b_macro_f1: 0.820\n      repo_4b_accuracy: 0.823\n      repo_8b_pass_f1: 0.841\n      repo_8b_fail_f1: 0.854\n      repo_8b_macro_f1: 0.848\n      repo_8b_accuracy: 0.850\n      context_length_median: 6842\n      context_length_p90: 14207\n      context_truncation_rate: 0.083\n      function_to_repo_f1_drop: 5.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Repo-level per-class F1 at 4B is 82.0 and at 8B is 84.8, both within the best-tier range\n      [80, 86]. The ~5.5-point drop from function-level (87.5) to repo-level (82.0) at 4B reflects\n      three factors: (1) longer contexts (median 6.8K tokens vs ~500 for function-level) make\n      attention less focused; (2) repo-level patches involve cross-file dependencies that are harder\n      to reason about; (3) context truncation affects 8.3% of cases where the full (patch, test)\n      pair exceeds the model's context window. Despite the drop, 82% F1 at repo-level is strong\n      enough to demonstrate the classification formulation transfers beyond function-level benchmarks.\n      The 8B model's improvement to 84.8% shows scaling helps more at repo-level (3-point gain vs\n      2.5-point at function-level), consistent with longer contexts benefiting from more capacity.\n      Per-class F1 ≥80% satisfies the best-tier threshold for all configurations.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 15: Repo-Level Classification Results (SWE-bench-PerTest)\"\n        columns: [\"Model\", \"Pass F1\", \"Fail F1\", \"Macro F1\", \"Accuracy\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Qwen3-4B\", \"81.2%\", \"82.8%\", \"82.0%\", \"82.3%\"]\n          - [\"Qwen3-8B\", \"84.1%\", \"85.4%\", \"84.8%\", \"85.0%\"]\n      - name: \"Table 16: Function-Level vs Repo-Level Gap\"\n        columns: [\"Level\", \"4B Macro F1\", \"8B Macro F1\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Function-level\", \"87.5%\", \"90.0%\"]\n          - [\"Repo-level\", \"82.0%\", \"84.8%\"]\n          - [\"Gap\", \"−5.5 pp\", \"−5.2 pp\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 13: Repo-level F1 as a function of input context length, showing graceful degradation beyond 8K tokens but still above 75% F1 at 16K+.\"\n    analysis: >\n      Repo-level results are the critical generalization test. The ≥80% F1 validates that the\n      classification formulation isn't limited to toy function-level benchmarks. The consistent\n      ~5.5-point gap between function and repo level across model sizes suggests this is a domain\n      difficulty effect, not a formulation limitation. The context-length degradation analysis\n      (Figure 13) provides actionable guidance on when to expect reduced accuracy and could\n      motivate future work on context-efficient architectures.\n\n  - name: \"LLM-as-Judge Baselines\"\n    plan_id: \"llm-judge-baseline\"\n    description: >\n      Zero-shot Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-32B as LLM judges for both trajectory-level and per-test\n      verification. Provides reference points for the accuracy-cost Pareto frontier.\n    expected_results:\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_traj_accuracy: 0.683\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_traj_f1: 0.671\n      qwen_8b_zeroshot_pertest_macro_f1: 0.634\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_traj_accuracy: 0.741\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_traj_f1: 0.728\n      qwen_32b_zeroshot_pertest_macro_f1: 0.702\n      qwen_8b_cost_per_instance: 0.003\n      qwen_32b_cost_per_instance: 0.012\n      finetuned_4b_cost_per_instance: 0.001\n    result_rationale: >\n      Zero-shot LLM judges perform substantially below fine-tuned classifiers because execution\n      simulation is not well-represented in pretraining data — models can reason about code\n      semantics but haven't learned to reliably predict pass/fail outcomes without training signal.\n      Qwen3-8B zero-shot achieves 67.1% trajectory F1, below even the CWM generation model (72.1%),\n      because it lacks the fine-tuning that teaches output format and calibration. Qwen3-32B improves\n      to 72.8% F1, roughly matching CWM at 4B — showing that 8× more parameters in zero-shot mode\n      barely matches 4B fine-tuned generation. Per-test zero-shot is worse than trajectory-level\n      zero-shot because models struggle to reason about individual test semantics without training.\n      These baselines establish that fine-tuning is essential (not just prompting) and that the\n      fine-tuned 4B classifier dominates the Pareto frontier (highest accuracy at lowest cost).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 17: Pareto Frontier — Accuracy vs Cost\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Params\", \"Fine-tuned\", \"Macro F1\", \"Cost/Instance\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"LLM-Judge ZS\", \"8B\", \"No\", \"67.1%\", \"$0.003\"]\n          - [\"LLM-Judge ZS\", \"32B\", \"No\", \"72.8%\", \"$0.012\"]\n          - [\"CWM Generation\", \"4B\", \"Yes\", \"72.1%\", \"$0.001\"]\n          - [\"Classification\", \"4B\", \"Yes\", \"87.5%\", \"$0.001\"]\n          - [\"Classification\", \"8B\", \"Yes\", \"90.0%\", \"$0.002\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Figure 14: Pareto frontier plot (x=cost per instance, y=macro F1) showing fine-tuned classification dominating zero-shot LLM judges across the cost spectrum.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The LLM-judge baselines serve two roles: (1) they show fine-tuning is necessary, ruling out\n      the \"just prompt a big model\" alternative; (2) they populate the Pareto frontier to demonstrate\n      fine-tuned classification is cost-optimal. For EMNLP reviewers, this also connects to the\n      broader NLP question of when task-specific fine-tuning beats general-purpose prompting — our\n      answer is \"for structured prediction tasks like execution simulation, fine-tuning a small\n      model decisively beats prompting a large one.\"\n\n  - name: \"Per-test Majority Voting (K=3)\"\n    plan_id: \"per-test-majority-voting\"\n    description: >\n      Run classification model 3 times per test with temperature sampling, take majority vote.\n      Tests if self-consistency improves per-test accuracy at 3× inference cost.\n    expected_results:\n      k3_pass_f1: 0.886\n      k3_fail_f1: 0.893\n      k3_macro_f1: 0.890\n      k3_accuracy: 0.891\n      single_pass_macro_f1: 0.875\n      improvement_from_voting: 1.5\n      cost_multiplier: 3.0\n      disagreement_rate: 0.134\n    result_rationale: >\n      Majority voting at K=3 provides a modest 1.5-point F1 improvement (87.5 → 89.0) at 3×\n      inference cost. The improvement is small because the base classifier is already well-calibrated\n      at 87.5% — there's limited room for self-consistency to correct errors. The 13.4% disagreement\n      rate (cases where not all 3 runs agree) identifies the model's uncertain predictions, which\n      could be useful for selective abstention. However, the cost-accuracy tradeoff (1.5 pp for 3×\n      cost) is unfavorable compared to simply using an 8B model (2.5 pp for ~2× cost). This\n      experiment is included as a P2 result for completeness but is not central to any claim.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1 (Appendix): Majority Voting Results\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Macro F1\", \"Relative Cost\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Single-pass (4B)\", \"87.5%\", \"1×\"]\n          - [\"Majority K=3 (4B)\", \"89.0%\", \"3×\"]\n          - [\"Single-pass (8B)\", \"90.0%\", \"~2×\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      Appendix-level result. Shows self-consistency helps marginally but model scaling is a more\n      efficient path to higher accuracy. The disagreement rate is potentially useful as a confidence\n      calibration signal in deployment but is not explored further in this paper.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"bd3967e0-8c52-4a08-9b3d-3623462e92c4","total_cost_usd":0.375308,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9894,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":12103,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9894,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12103,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9894},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4850,"outputTokens":23,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004965,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":12103,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9894,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.370343,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"b99bd79a-1c77-4882-bbb1-8e4bd5c8f7f4"}
